  "title": "Uniform Random Variable",
  "body": " Uniform Random Variable   This section is different from the others in this chapter. Here you will learn about the discrete uniform random variable through a set of written and interactive exercises. (Complete the written exercises first. Do not move on to the interactive problems until you have completed the written part.) You will ultimately derive its probability distribution function and other properties.    Written Exercises   Rolling a Die   Suppose you roll a single, fair, 6-sided die. Let the random variable represent the number that is rolled.      Find the probability distribution function, , for the random variable .        Compute the expected value, , and the variance, , for the random variable .        The discrete uniform random variable is used to model experiments where there are a finite number, , of equally likely outcomes. Based on this definition, explain why the random variable can be considered a discrete uniform random variable.        Provide another real-world scenario where the variable of interest would be a discrete uniform random variable. Fully describe it here. Make sure to define the random variable and provide its probability distribution. Also, explain why your random variable would be considered uniform.        Write a general formula that can be used for the probability distribution function of any discrete uniform random variable. Assume the random variable takes on values 1 through . Explain the reasoning behind your answer.        Show that the expected value and variance for a discrete uniform random variable, that takes on values 1 through , can be expressed as:   Use your generalized probability distribution function from from part (e) to analytically derive the theoretical formulas.    You may want to review your summation formulas for common sequences\/series, e.g., the geometric series. I have provided two more examples below.          Interactive Exercises   Derivation of the MGF   Let $X$ be a discrete uniform random variable on the support , with probability distribution function . Derive the moment generating function for .  Reorder the blocks below to derive . (Go step by step! Don't skip or combine steps.) You may or may not use all provided blocks.    Recall the geometric series, specifically the sum of the first n terms:      By definition:     Substitute into the summation:     Reindex the summation to start at zero:     Rewrite the summation to look like a geometric series (see hint):     Apply the finite geometric series formula:     Simplify to arrive at the final expression:       Tetris Game Mechanics   Recall the tile-matching block game Tetris, that utilizes a 7-bag random generator. The algorithm places exactly one copy of each of the seven unique shapes ( I, J, L, O, S, T, Z ) into a bag, shuffles them completely, dispenses all seven, and then immediately repeats the process with a fresh bag.     Let be the position of the -piece in the first 7-bag that is generated. Random variable therefore ranges from 1 to 7. Is a uniform random variable?    The finite number of outcomes are equally likely.       A player tracks the game queue across two consecutive 7-bags (bag 1 followed by bag 2). Similar to , define to be the position of the -piece in the second 7-bag that is generated. The random variables and are independent.    The two 7-bags are treated as completely independent in the system, so the positioning of the -piece in the second 7-bag will not be affected by the position of the -piece in the first 7-bag.      Now define random variable to be the spawn distance between the first bag's -piece and the second bag's -piece. [For example, if the first -piece is the sixth shape in bag 1, and the next -piece is the second shape in bag 2, the distance is pieces.]  Determine the finite support space for the random variable . In other words, what values can take on? Fill in the blanks below to answer this question. Your answers should be integers  Minimum distance:  piece(s) apart  Maximum distance:  piece(s) apart                can be expressed as a linear combination of and : .  Is a uniform random variable? Or in other words, is a linear combination of uniform random variables also itself uniform? Explain why or why not.     Yes, because linear combinations of independent random variables keep the same distribution type.  Incorrect. Combining two independent uniform random variables does not yield another uniform random variable. (Also, think about the Binomial random variable, which is a linear combination of Bernoulli random variables.)    No, because different values of are associated with a varying number of combinations of and .  Correct! Extreme values like can only happen 1 way, while middle values can happen in multiple ways, creating a non-uniform probability distribution.    No, because and are independent.  Incorrect. While it is true that is not unfiorm, the reasoning is unrelated to the relationship between and .    Yes, because takes on a finite number of values.  Incorrect. While it is true that takes on a finite number of values, the probabilities for each value are not the same.        Calculate the probability of experiencing the minimum distance gap. Express your answer as a decimal rounded to two decimal places.  Probability =       Correct! There is exactly favorable pair out of the possible independent arrangements.         How far apart would you expect the -pieces to be on average? In other words, what is the expected spawn gap?    piece(s) apart.      Correct! By linearity of expectation: . Since both uniforms random variables have an expected value of 4, .         Calculate the probability of experiencing a spawn distance gap of exactly seven pieces, .      Incorrect. This is the probability for the edge cases ( or ).     Incorrect. This would apply to a distance gap of 3 or 11.     Correct! The condition implies . There are exactly 7 matching pairs: out of 49 total outcomes.     Incorrect. This would apply to a distance gap of 6 or 8.     Incorrect. This would apply to a distance gap of 5 or 9.       "
