  "body": " Direct Sales   Your professor is annoyed by pushy door-to-door salespeople who walk through neighborhoods offering house painting services. Let's say that the probability of a salesperson securing a customer agreement at any individual home is a constant (or 10%). Assume that all individual household interactions behave as completely independent trials.      Compute the probability that the salesperson secures their first customer agreement exactly at the 10th house they visit. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.  Try this two ways - using the geometric distribution and the negative binomial distribution. Remember that the geometric distribution is just a special case of the negative binomial where !           Correct! This calculation can be viewed in two identical ways:  Geometric:  Negative Binomial with :            Compute the probability that it requires visiting exactly 60 houses in order to successfully close 7 painting agreements. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.           Correct! This follows a Negative Binomial model where the th success occurs precisely on trial :           Compute the probability that the salesperson receives exactly 53 rejections before the 7th painting agreement is completed. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.           Correct! This is identical to Part B, because 53 rejections plus 7 successful agreements equals exactly 60 total household visits.          How many total houses do you expect the salesperson will have to visit in order to successfully accumulate a quota of exactly 25 painting agreements? Write your answer as a whole number.           Correct! Using the expected value formula for Negative Binomial:           If the salesperson gets a $250 bonus for each painting agreement secured, what is the mean number of household visits required to hit a bonus target of $3,000?    houses   Incorrect. This is the value of , the number of successful painting agreements required to hit $3,000 ( ). You still need to calculate the expected number of total household visits required to land that many sales.   houses   Incorrect. It looks like you divided the entire $3,000 revenue target by the probability parameter . Make sure to convert your revenue target to a target number of successful sales ( ) first.   houses   Correct! First, determine the number of target successes ( ) needed to reach the financial goal: Next, apply the expected value function using our parameter count of and success probability :   houses   Incorrect. It looks like you multiplied the target sales number of 12 by the probability parameter instead of dividing by it. The expected number of trials will always be larger than the target number of successes when .   houses   Incorrect. This is the number of failures before reaching the target number of successes.     "
