  "body": " Capture-Recapture Scenario   Suppose there are 1,000 fish in a lake. During the first week, 65 fish are captured, tagged, and released. Then, 30 fish are captured two weeks later. Let be a hypergeometric random variable representing the number of tagged fish found in the sample of size 30.      What is the probability that 3 of the 30 fish will be tagged? In other words, use the hypergeometric probability distribution to find .        Consider the second sample of size 30. Compute each of the following probabilities. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places.  Remember, the sample is taken without replacement .      What is the probability that the first fish captured is tagged?         Suppose that the first fish was tagged. What is the probability that the second fish captured is also tagged?         Suppose that the first fish was tagged but the second fish was not. What is the probability that the third fish captured is tagged?         Suppose 29 fish have been caught and that two of these fish were tagged. What is the probability that the last fish captured (number 30 in our sample) is tagged?          Referring to part (b), explain why these trials can be considered Bernoulli trials but not independent and identically distributed Bernoulli trials (which we need in order to use the Binomial distribution).       Because the sample size is small relative to the size of the population (30 vs 1,000), the probabilities you calculated in part (b) are quite similar!  Is it therefore reasonable to just fix the probability of success at for all 30 trials (and pretend they are independent)? If so, this would allow us to use the binomial distribution!  Determine whether the binomial distribution can provide an accurate approximation for the probability in part (a). In other words, re-compute the probability that exactly 3 of the 30 fish will be tagged using the binomial distribution with . Then, compare your answer to your initial answer using the hypergeometric distribution.        In general, if the sample size is at most 5% of the population, then we can use the binomial distribution to approximate the hypergeometric.  Is the sample size ( )for the capture-recapture scenario less than 5% of the population? Justify your answer.    "
